# Architecture

`App UI/domain code -> generated manager or IPC bridge -> maintained runtime contract -> upstream SDK`

Desktop: Electron main embeds Pear Runtime; sandboxed renderer uses preload/IPC; Bare worker owns P2P, storage and the AI session. Node: app manager owns a session with the Node adapter; QVAC manages its own worker. Expo: React Native hosts its pinned QVAC SDK, while the separate Bare worklet hosts networking/storage. Do not move QVAC into the app's mobile networking worklet.

The runtime is shipped at `.unstoppable/runtime-ai/` with a recorded version. Generated apps own their copy until migrated; edit prompts/UI in app code. Avoid copying new engine setup into each feature. Use direct advanced SDK access inside a runtime boundary only after checking installed types and native support.

Storage: prefer `@unstoppable-stack/p2p/data` for new shared JSON: `openCollection` for one writer, `openCollaborativeDatabase` for approved writers. Read `modules/p2p.mdc` and `modules/managed-data.mdc`. Preserve existing generated Corestore/Hyperbee or Autobee/HyperDB stores and deterministic merge policy. Schemas and domain policy belong to the app. Keep private state outside replication.

Use `@unstoppable-stack/p2p/service` and `/rpc` for new remote services; root `createNode` handles multiple packs or bidirectional calls. The library owns discovery, identity, pairing, grants and transport; do not rebuild them with raw Hyperswarm/bare-rpc wrappers. `/sync` hosts existing cores/drives. No Personal Node is required. The deferred AI capability pack is a separate P2P exposure and is not the local inference session.

The app manifest and local compatibility profiles distinguish preview, experimental and deferred combinations. A scaffold does not certify support. Select guidance for the installed profile before reading upstream docs.
